TCP urgent data OOM: a paused peer bypasses the receive flood limit.

Disclosed 2026-10-28 ("Unbounded memory growth through TCP urgent data on
non-Linux systems", fixed upstream in PR #36665, Bitcoin Core 29.5, 30.4,
31.2 and 32.1). No CVE assigned at time of writing.

ThreadSocketHandler() stops select()ing a peer's socket for reading once its
receive queue exceeds ReceiveFloodSize(), but it always added the socket to
fdsetError. Any socket in fdsetError is then serviced with recv():

    if (FD_ISSET(pnode->hSocket, &fdsetRecv) || FD_ISSET(pnode->hSocket, &fdsetError))

TCP urgent (out-of-band) data raises the exceptional condition, so a peer can
keep sending urgent bytes to force reads while it is supposed to be paused,
growing vRecvMsg without bound until the node is OOM-killed.

Upstream says Linux is unaffected because modern Core uses poll() there.
0.12.1 uses select() on every platform, including Linux, so this image is
affected.

Fix: only watch for exceptional conditions when the socket is also being
watched for reading. Sockets that are draining their send queue or paused for
receive are left out of fdsetError, so they can no longer trigger a recv().

diff --git src/net.cpp src/net.cpp
index 7baad38209..28d460aa7d 100644
--- src/net.cpp
+++ src/net.cpp
@@ -1135,7 +1135,6 @@ void ThreadSocketHandler()
             {
                 if (pnode->hSocket == INVALID_SOCKET)
                     continue;
-                FD_SET(pnode->hSocket, &fdsetError);
                 hSocketMax = max(hSocketMax, pnode->hSocket);
                 have_fds = true;
 
@@ -1165,8 +1164,14 @@ void ThreadSocketHandler()
                     TRY_LOCK(pnode->cs_vRecvMsg, lockRecv);
                     if (lockRecv && (
                         pnode->vRecvMsg.empty() || !pnode->vRecvMsg.front().complete() ||
-                        pnode->GetTotalRecvSize() <= ReceiveFloodSize()))
+                        pnode->GetTotalRecvSize() <= ReceiveFloodSize())) {
                         FD_SET(pnode->hSocket, &fdsetRecv);
+                        // TCP urgent data OOM: only watch for exceptional
+                        // conditions while receiving is allowed. An exceptional
+                        // condition triggers recv() below, so watching it while
+                        // reads are paused let a peer bypass ReceiveFloodSize().
+                        FD_SET(pnode->hSocket, &fdsetError);
+                    }
                 }
             }
         }
